{
  "teamId": 147,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 New York Yankees entered the season with sky-high expectations and quickly found their bullpen to be a double-edged sword. Early on, closer Clay Holmes proved to be one of the most volatile figures on the roster, surrendering key hits and home runs at critical moments while the offense struggled to find consistent footing. April and May were a rollercoaster, with Anthony Volpe and Giancarlo Stanton providing electric moments but Holmes repeatedly unraveling late-game leads, including a brutal June 13th meltdown against Kansas City that cost the Yankees dearly.\n\nThe summer months brought a surge of optimism. Juan Soto's mammoth June home run reminded the baseball world why New York had mortgaged so much to acquire him, and the July trade deadline delivered Jazz Chisholm Jr., who announced his arrival with a three-run blast that electrified the Bronx. Rookie Ben Rice delivered a stunning three-run homer to salvage a game in mid-July, and Aaron Judge continued his relentless march toward historic numbers, launching his 52nd home run — a grand slam — in September. The Yankees steadied themselves, won 94 games, and rolled into October with genuine championship swagger.\n\nThe Championship Series against Cleveland was a white-knuckle affair. Judge and Soto delivered when it mattered most, but the bullpen — Holmes and Weaver alike — continued to flicker in the high-stakes moments, surrendering crucial home runs that made each game feel like a coin flip. Soto's three-run blast in Game 3 kept the Yankees alive and reminded everyone what this lineup was capable of when it clicked together under October pressure.\n\nThe World Series against the Los Angeles Dodgers was where the Yankees' season came to its heartbreaking end. Stanton's two-run homer in Game 1 felt like destiny, but Freddie Freeman's catastrophic grand slam off Nestor Cortes swung the series in Los Angeles's favor in a moment that will echo through Yankee lore for years. Anthony Volpe's own grand slam in the final game was a defiant, emotional swing — a sign of what this young core might become — but it wasn't enough. The 2024 Yankees were a team that flashed brilliance and crumbled at the edges, a champion in waiting that ran out of October outs.",
